artifact.self_contained_problem:
Consider a quantum network in which end nodes share entangled quantum states (e.g., EPR pairs) generated and distributed across intermediate nodes, and in which qubits are stored in finite-capacity quantum memories. The transport layer is responsible for end-to-end delivery of entanglement and quantum data between communicating endpoints. Two physical constraints govern the design of any such layer:

(i) the no-cloning theorem, which forbids creating an independent copy of an unknown quantum state, so that lost or discarded quantum information cannot be regenerated by retransmission of a copy; and
(ii) the difficulty of establishing end-to-end entanglement, which requires successful entanglement generation, swapping, and (possibly) distillation across multiple links subject to decoherence, memory decay, and limited rates.

In current proposed quantum transport-layer protocols, when congestion arises (e.g., insufficient memory, queue overflow, or link contention), entangled qubits and quantum data are discarded — analogous to packet drops in classical TCP/IP. Because of (i)–(ii), such discarding entails irrecoverable loss of resources that were costly to produce.

Problem. Design a quantum transport-layer congestion-control mechanism that:
(a) responds to congestion (memory exhaustion, link bottlenecks, fidelity decay below a threshold) without discarding entangled states or quantum data whenever feasible;
(b) respects the no-cloning theorem (i.e., does not rely on copying unknown quantum states for retransmission);
(c) coordinates with lower layers (link/network) so that already-established end-to-end entanglement is preserved or productively reused (for example via buffering, rerouting, entanglement swapping/relocation, distillation, or graceful degradation of fidelity);
(d) provides provable guarantees on throughput, fidelity, and stability of the resulting protocol under realistic models of memory decoherence and stochastic entanglement generation.

Formulate such a protocol and analyze its performance, or prove fundamental impossibility bounds showing the extent to which discarding of entangled states is unavoidable under congestion.
